1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,120
Hi, I'm Tom from the Burp Scanner development team. Today I'm going to talk a little bit about

2
00:00:06,120 --> 00:00:11,280
insertion point relocation. So Burp Scanner during the crawl is trying to find paths through an

3
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:15,240
application to each request that it will then want to audit so that when we go back to that

4
00:00:15,240 --> 00:00:19,560
request it's valid, everything's up to date, it's all in session, sort of like automatic session

5
00:00:19,560 --> 00:00:24,160
handling. This means that when we are resending our base requests during the audit they might

6
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:32,040
change slightly. Things like cookies or session tokens or cash busters might update with each

7
00:00:32,040 --> 00:00:37,240
time we send the request and if anything we kind of expect them to. That's why we're doing our

8
00:00:37,240 --> 00:00:41,840
automatic session handling to make sure they're valid and fresh and up to date each time. The

9
00:00:41,840 --> 00:00:46,780
problem with this is that as that request changes the part of the request that we're actually trying

10
00:00:46,780 --> 00:00:55,960
to put our payload in might need to move slightly. So for example this foo parameter here is after

11
00:00:55,960 --> 00:01:02,560
this token parameter here with the value x. If this value changed length each time this base request was

12
00:01:02,560 --> 00:01:08,860
resubmitted then the foo parameter would kind of move slightly to the right each time its offsets would

13
00:01:08,860 --> 00:01:13,820
change. If we just use hard-coded offsets for our insertion points then we could end up placing our

14
00:01:13,820 --> 00:01:19,520
payload in completely the wrong place resulting in a malformed request. Instead, Burp Scanner understands

15
00:01:19,520 --> 00:01:24,860
the features of the insertion point it's using and can dynamically relocate each time. So to

16
00:01:24,860 --> 00:01:32,180
demonstrate this I've put together this really simple web application. It's got two pages. A first page

17
00:01:32,180 --> 00:01:38,940
that has a single form on it. This form each time we visit it has this hidden token parameter with the value

18
00:01:38,940 --> 00:01:46,440
of x and with each visit this parameter value gets slightly longer. When the form is submitted that

19
00:01:46,440 --> 00:01:52,720
parameter is validated and if it's not of the right length we reject the form submission. So if I send this

20
00:01:52,720 --> 00:01:59,320
to repeater because I've already done a scan of this that length isn't valid anymore. So I previously run a

21
00:01:59,320 --> 00:02:05,960
scan of this application and if we head over to the logger we can see that each request has a payload

22
00:02:05,960 --> 00:02:11,080
in the place in the correct spot for the value of the parameter we're attacking. So with each of these

23
00:02:11,640 --> 00:02:18,840
even though the parameter is moving we're putting our payload in the correct place. Burp Scanner can

24
00:02:18,840 --> 00:02:24,040
also handle insertion point index changing. So for example if those two parameters swapped order and it

25
00:02:24,040 --> 00:02:29,720
can also handle insertion point name changes. I wanted to keep the demo nice and simple so I didn't cover

26
00:02:29,720 --> 00:02:34,520
either of those. So you'll just have to take my word for it or you could try it out yourself.

27
00:02:35,960 --> 00:02:39,640
So you can see that.

